  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "F5aZ+WfuTGVf+amfXOxUSF+w8zY=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:37.381Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "GONICUS delivers Sovereign Cloud Stack, Proxmox and Kubernetes. All three are infrastructure: they settle where workloads run, who operates them, and under whose jurisdiction the substrate sits. That is a real form of sovereignty and it is the layer the firm sells today.\n\nThe research's observation is that nothing in the portfolio supplies a sovereign business record above it. Controlling the substrate does not produce an attributed, replayable account of what an organisation decided \u2014 a self-hosted cluster can run an application whose decisions are exactly as unreconstructable as a hosted one's. The two layers are independent, and only one of them is covered.\n\nThat makes the fit a portfolio extension rather than a displacement, which is the easiest conversation to have with an infrastructure firm. Powerhouse is the application-layer counterpart to the argument GONICUS already makes to its customers, so the sovereignty case does not need to be established \u2014 only carried one layer up.\n\n---\n\n## Leads sharing this finding (1)\n\n**2 \u00b7 next**\n- GONICUS GmbH (DE Arnsberg/Bonn/Duesseldorf) \u2014 Stefan Grote + Rainer L\u00fclsdorf + Lars Scheiter \u00b7 info@gonicus.de \u00b7 https://gonicus.de\n\nVerbatim research text this claim was extracted from:\n\n> They deliver Sovereign Cloud Stack, Proxmox and Kubernetes \u2014 sovereign INFRASTRUCTURE with no sovereign business record on top. Powerhouse is the application-layer counterpart to what they already sell.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-19T11:28:37.381815Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-19T11:28:37.381820Z"
    }
  },
